    "durationMs": 222353,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified. Everything passed: 44/44 valid checks (the one earlier \"fail\" was a bug in my own test script's expected count), plus restart persistence and concurrency checks.\n\n**What I built:**\n- **Stack**: Spring Boot 3.4.5 / Java 21, Spring Data JPA, Flyway, PostgreSQL driver — added to the provided pom.xml.\n- **Schema**: `V1__init.sql` creates a `links` table (unique code, url text, clicks counter, created_at); Flyway applies it at startup before serving, and applies nothing on reboots (verified).\n- **DB config**: an `EnvironmentPostProcessor` parses the `DATABASE_URL` env var (`postgresql://user:pass@host:port/db`) into JDBC datasource properties — no hard-coded credentials.\n- **API**: `POST /links` (201, generated or custom code, duplicate → 409), `GET /links/{code}` (404 on unknown), `GET /r/{code}` (302 + Location, atomic `UPDATE ... clicks = clicks + 1` so counts never lose updates), `GET /links` (ascending by creation with `total`). Malformed JSON, invalid/missing url, and invalid codes all return 422 with an `error` field.\n- **start.sh**: runs the prebuilt jar in the foreground on 0.0.0.0:8000; migrations are applied during app startup before Tomcat accepts connections. README documents startup.\n\n**Verification highlights**: all error/edge cases (boundary 4- and 20-char codes, uppercase schemes, url echo unchanged), 25 concurrent redirects incremented exactly 25, and links plus click counts survived a full server restart. Database was reset afterward and the server stopped, so the automated suite starts clean with `sh start.sh`."
